    {
      "id": 233956,
      "name": "drkrash1969",
      "username": "drkrash1969",
      "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/d/eada6e/{size}.png",
      "created_at": "2020-10-19T23:02:57.000Z",
      "cooked": "<p>While one of my players wasn't crazy about the issue structure (he's more or less just gotten over it), I've found it super-helpful for plotting stories, PROVIDED you want to follow a comic book structure (which is a big part of the whole game).&nbsp; In my longest Champions game, we played 8 hour sessions, but we modeled the game on soap operas and other TV rather than comics - one thing just ran into another and as long as we had a reason for a fight each session, it all worked.</p>\n<p>Now I'm planning scenes in discrete amounts to build a story and it's helped this old man a lot.&nbsp; Sometimes the planned events of an \"issue\" spill over into next session (in fact, they *usually* do), but I still account for an \"issue\" ending shortly into the next session.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
      "post_number": 4,
      "post_type": 1,
      "updated_at": "2021-02-07T06:09:55.071Z",
      "reply_count": 0,
      "reply_to_post_number": null,
      "quote_count": 0,
